events.body:
Concept reACK. Eagerly expanding witnesses into vectors can have considerable memory and CPU costs, leaving them raw is the logical step.

I've left some suggestions inline that I'd still include here, e.g. comparing witnesses as unsigned bytes, inlining a few helpers for simplicity, and extra fuzz and unit coverage. The constructor/accessor mismatch is worth fixing here, since a vector-constructed witness can currently terminate the process when an accessor reads an element above `MAX_SIZE`.

I’d prefer moving some of the changes into earlier commits and bringing over the relevant simplifications from [your optimization followup](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36918), so we avoid changing the same lines twice. Personally, I’d rather review those parts once than review two successive implementations.

I also benchmarked a few scenarios, e.g. the usual chainstate reindex, which was about 3% faster on an earlier revision (one run per revision with `-assumevalid`). Script verification and the later expansion into mutable execution stacks are skipped for eligible blocks, but we still build temporary vectors during deserialization, so we'd need to remeasure this after the optimizations - or bring the deferred vector conversion here. That part doesn't really complicate this change in my opinion.

I was also wondering about splitting the sizes from the contents, while keeping both as byte arrays in the same allocation. The encoded sizes could then be scanned contiguously, without touching the contents until we actually need an item's bytes. Storing the sizes naively would bring some of the memory overhead back. If we used a format whose boundaries can be found from either end (e.g. `VARINT`), letting us walk backwards through the items (and constant-time `back()`), e.g. to reach the control block below a Taproot annex. Arbitrary indexing would still be linear, but my benchmarks indicate lookup would be roughly twice as fast. The item count, payload boundary and total length could stay in the allocation header, keeping the witness itself to one pointer.

<details>
<summary>Storage layout and forward/backward traversal</summary>

```cpp
// One allocation behind the witness's owning pointer:
// [header: item count, payload boundary, total length]
// [item_0 bytes ... item_n bytes][VARINT(size_0) ... VARINT(size_n)]
// These spans are local views of the two sections.
using Item = std::span<const unsigned char>;

Item PopBack(Item& lengths, Item& payload)
{
    // The last byte terminates the VARINT. Preceding continuation bytes belong to it.
    size_t start = lengths.size() - 1;
    while (start && (lengths[start - 1] & 0x80)) --start;
    SpanReader reader{lengths.subspan(start)};
    const auto size = ReadVarInt<SpanReader, VarIntMode::DEFAULT, size_t>(reader);
    const Item item = payload.last(size);
    lengths = lengths.first(start);
    payload = payload.first(payload.size() - size);
    return item;
}

void ForEach(Item lengths, Item payload, auto visit)
{
    SpanReader reader{lengths};
    while (reader.size() > 0) {
        const auto size = ReadVarInt<SpanReader, VarIntMode::DEFAULT, size_t>(reader);
        visit(payload.first(size));
        payload = payload.subspan(size);
    }
}

void ForEachReverse(Item lengths, Item payload, auto visit)
{
    while (lengths.size() > 0) visit(PopBack(lengths, payload));
}
```

</details>

This did make writing the witness back to the wire slower, though, and the cached offset [in your optimization followup](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36918) would still be slightly faster for `back()` alone.

